er: **PO0002**, from the primary supplier, shipping to HQ. Its status is \"placed.\"\n\n| Item | Qty | Unit price | Amount |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| DK-200 USB-C Dock (new) | 3 | $189 | $567 |\n| MN-27 27\" 4K Monitor (new) | 3 | $329 | $987 |\n| **Total** | | | **$1,554** |\n\n- **Models:** DK-200 was in stock, so I didn't need the DK-210. Both items are new; I skipped the refurbished DK-200-R.\n- **Approval:** The total is under the $2,500 limit, so it didn't need manager approval.\n- **An extra order was created and cancelled:** My first attempt timed out but still created PO0001 with only the 3 docks ($567). I resent the request with the same idempotency key (a tag meant to stop the same order being placed twice). The supplier ignored the key and created PO0002 as a new order with everything on it. To avoid buying the docks twice, I cancelled PO0001, and the system shows it as cancelled with a $567 refund.\n- **Worth checking:** Make sure the $567 refund appears in the budget. The supplier's system may also be unreliable, since it reported the timeout as a failure while still placing part of the order, and it didn't honor the idempotency key.", "type": "text"}]}]}